{#
  ALSA alias for a Bluetooth room output, managed by Ansible (roles/snapclient).
  snapclient plays into the PCM named "snaphouse_speaker"; bluealsa routes it over
  A2DP to this room's speaker. The plug layer converts snapclient's stream to
  whatever the A2DP link negotiates.

  The name is snaphouse_speaker, not snaphouse_bt: snapclient resolves its
  --soundcard by a name match, and no snaphouse PCM name may be a prefix of
  another (the hub also defines snaphouse_bt_input for the Bluetooth loopback
  feed), or the output could bind the input PCM and feed the hub's output back
  into itself.

  The speaker's address is the household's, so it is read from the untracked
  /etc/snaphouse/local.yml (speakers.<name>) at apply time and never written to
  the repo. `speakers` is loaded from local.yml by site.yml.

  Until the speaker is paired its address is empty. A bluealsa device with an
  empty address must never be written, so nothing is rendered until an address
  exists; keepalive keeps snapclient stopped in the meantime, and `snaphouse pair
  speaker` prompts for a re-apply once the address is recorded.
#}
{% set speaker = snaphouse_hosts[snaphouse_host].output.speaker %}
{% set mac = (speakers | default({})).get(speaker) %}
{% if mac %}
pcm.snaphouse_speaker {
    type plug
    slave.pcm {
        type bluealsa
        device "{{ mac }}"
        profile "a2dp"
    }
    hint {
        show on
        description "snaphouse Bluetooth speaker"
    }
}
{% else %}
# No address for the {{ speaker }} speaker in /etc/snaphouse/local.yml yet, so no
# Bluetooth output PCM is defined. Pair it (snaphouse pair speaker {{ speaker }})
# and re-apply; keepalive keeps snapclient stopped until then.
{% endif %}
